  "commit": "c8aad71482755fa09421d5914cda05a3b46c3321",
  "description": "THE ITERATION AN ADVERSARIAL CONSULTATION PAID FOR. run/0021 closed on its own ledger pass; this run is everything that followed from asking a Fable agent to attack the belief that the remaining v1 work is entirely owner-gated. Four of its findings were verified against the tree first-hand before anything was touched, and three of the four were worse than the report said.\n\nWHAT THE MERGES ARE. #374 rewrote the operations runbook's Trash section, which told an operator the opposite of the code on the one irreversible operation in the product: trash purge refuses BECAUSE a period is archived, where period purge REQUIRES it, there is no retention window at that door at all, and the directory purge the page described does not exist. #376 gave Run.merges a field pair per main-push workflow and a guard that refuses if the set drifts. #378 deleted a directory literally named `undefined` that had been shipping 16,681 unreferenced bytes into every image layer, and made the class refuse. #379 corrected four texts the tree contradicts, two of them worse than reported.\n\nTHE RUN BOUNDARY IS ITSELF A FINDING, recorded in run/0021's closing note: nothing triggers it, so it drifts open until somebody decides. This run is opened deliberately rather than inherited, and startTime is the loop trigger's own firing time (2026-09-21T20:44:58Z) rather than a round number -- 23 seconds after run/0021's close, which is also what makes it strictly after, as the validator requires.\n\nMY OWN TWO MISTAKES THIS RUN, both caught by CI on #376 and both worth the space. I inserted a constant BETWEEN checkMergeEntry()'s docblock and its signature, orphaning the `@param list<string>` and costing phpstan the by-ref type at the call site. And I rewrote two blocks that .github/mutations.json registers by exact source text, which mutation-check caught and named precisely. The second was repointed, not weakened: same expectFailing tests, and each new `remove` text was applied to a copy of the validator and shown to silence it -- because \"I updated the registry to match\" is the sentence a weakened guard would also produce.\n\nTHE CLAUSE SWEEP LOOP STEP 4 MANDATES, RUN IN FULL AND REPORTED EMPTY. Every clause of every open v1 item -- 682 lines across six items, eligible or not -- read rather than summarised, because the item-granularity reading has been wrong seven times in this project and the previous iteration reported \"all owner-gated\" at exactly that granularity. It holds at clause granularity too: item/release-v0-1-0's three proofs, item/image-size's \"printed by the release job\", item/readme-owner's clause 2, item/v1-audit's clause (c) subject and item/tag-v1-0-0's clauses (1)(2)(3) all need one tag push; item/docs-site needs two repository settings. Nothing buildable was found, which is what makes a ledger-only pull request permitted here.\n\nTHE SWEEP PAID FOR ITSELF ANYWAY, twice. item/tag-v1-0-0's clause (1) records that mutation/0057 exists to prove widening assert-release-gate.py's edge list did not weaken the edge decision/0120 put there -- and PR #366 widened it again, with two more edges and a gate-equality rule, without re-running that mutant. Re-run here: dropping boot-published-image still exits 1, and so does dropping image -> changelog-section. The guarantee survives, now checked rather than inherited. And the sweep turned up that every release.yml line citation in the ledger had rotted, which is this pass's other half.\n\nTHE COMPOSE STACK BOOTED FOR THE FIRST TIME, 2026-09-22. Every `docker compose` invocation CI had ever run was `config`, which renders and interpolates the file and starts nothing -- so compose-secret proved the secret is required and proved nothing about whether the stack runs, while CLAUDE.md calls `docker compose up -d --build` \"the real stack\" and quick-start.md offers it as a first-class install path.\n\nI ALSO OVER-BLOCKED MYSELF ON IT, one iteration earlier, and the correction is the reusable part. I reported the whole item blocked on issue #382, having conflated what the DOCS should say (the owner's) with what CI should ASSERT (mine). Booting the default stack and asserting the app answers needs no product decision at all; only the sibling-MinIO half does. That is LOOP step 6's standing lesson one level up -- a refused command establishes that THAT command was refused, and an open decision establishes that THAT decision is open, not that everything touching it is blocked.\n\nMAIN WENT RED AT 7fa7fb5 AND WAS GREEN AGAIN AT 71cd528, one cycle later. The cause was the compose-boot job added an hour earlier, and it was neither the job nor a flake: all four default-stack services mount the same db: volume at /data, `docker compose up` starts them within a millisecond of each other, and two race on `mkdir /data/caddy` -- the loser fails to start with `file exists`. The PR run won that race and main's run lost it. Issue #385 carries the log.\n\nTHE RED MERGE IS RECORDED AS `failure` rather than smoothed over. Rule 3 requires only the run's LAST merge to close green, which is what makes an honest red in the middle recordable at all.\n\nWHAT THE INCIDENT IS EVIDENCE FOR, stated because the easy reading is the wrong one: not that the job was bad, but that the compose path had never been executed and therefore had an unobserved race in it. CLAUDE.md's \"several bugs in this codebase were invisible to a fully green suite\" is the whole argument, and this is an instance of it arriving on schedule. What the job cost was one red main for eleven minutes; what it bought is a defect in the documented install path that nobody would otherwise have seen until a self-hoster hit it.\n\nTHE RACE WAS DIAGNOSED WRONG THREE TIMES AND FIXED ON THE FOURTH READING, 2026-09-22, in PR #388. The `mkdir /data/caddy: file exists` failure is the DOCKER DAEMON'S, seeding a fresh named volume by copying the image's content at the mount path -- not any entrypoint's. Three of the four default-stack services override `command:` in compose.yaml, so they never run the image's CMD and never start Caddy at all; the configuration has exactly one possible runner of that code and a race needs two. Everything above in this description, in issue #385's body and in the revert commit that says \"each entrypoint creates its runtime directories under /data\" is wrong about the actor, and all of it is mine. decision/0133 is what that cost and what would have caught it: enumerate the actors a configuration permits before assigning one to an error message that names an operation but no caller.\n\nTHE FIX IS THAT THE IMAGE MUST SHIP NOTHING UNDER /data. `rm -rf /data && mkdir -p /data && chown www-data:www-data /data`: it must still exist so a fresh volume inherits its ownership, and empty there is nothing for the daemon to copy. XDG_DATA_HOME is left alone, which also settles the option I had recommended on #385 and had explicitly marked unverified -- whether relocating it would strand anything. Read from source rather than assumed: the base image sets SSL_MODE=off and CADDY_AUTO_HTTPS=off, doccum overrides neither, ssl-mode/off.caddyfile has one plain http site block and imports no tls directive, and caddy.go's InstanceID() regenerates instance.uuid when absent. Nothing durable, and relocating would have been the one option capable of stranding certificates for anyone running SSL_MODE=full.\n\nAND storage.md'S SPEC 13 CLAIM IS VINDICATED, not refuted as #385 suspected: the four containers DO start independently. The claim simply had an unstated requirement, which PR #388 states next to it and CI now enforces.\n\nWHAT PROVES THE FIX IS NOT THE JOB THAT FOUND THE BUG. compose-boot returns to main with the fix, as the removal commit promised, but a green run of a racy path is a lucky run -- this job's first two runs demonstrated exactly that on identical code. The proof is a deterministic step in the `image` job that REFUSES an image shipping content under /data, per decision/0113, shown failing by mutation/0060 with the Dockerfile's `rm -rf /data` taken back out. decision/0134 generalises it: guard the property an intermittent defect depends on, keep the integration test as a witness.\n\nISSUE #382'S OWN PROPOSAL WAS ALSO CHECKED AND FOUND NOT TO WORK, which changes what half two of item/compose-boot-ci is blocked on. Compose does not let the host shell override a literal `environment:` value, so the command block that issue proposes renders `DOCCUM_EMBEDDED_STORAGE: \"true\"` on app and `AWS_ENDPOINT: http://app:9000` on all three workers regardless (Compose v5.1.1, output posted on #382). The real choice is between changing compose.yaml to interpolate those two values and documenting the two-step honestly -- not between two documentation edits, as the issue framed it.\n\nTHE CONSULTATION'S LAST FOUR FINDINGS, CLEARED, 2026-09-22, in PR #403 -- and three of them were the same shape: a document asserting a property of the ledger that nothing enforced. item/claims-without-guards carries the detail; what belongs here is what the shape costs.\n\nA CLAIM THAT HAPPENS TO BE TRUE READS EXACTLY LIKE A CLAIM THAT IS ENFORCED. vocab.md said the merge-record keys \"are always present\" and they were, on all 286 entries, held there by habit; checkMergeEntry() read each one as `$entry[$field] ?? null`, which cannot tell an absent key from a recorded null. Nothing notices a deleted node either -- mutation/0053 left a hole in the sequence and every rule stepped over it. LOOP.md said only item status is mutable while decision/0106 requires doneWhen clauses to be amended. Each verified first-hand before being written down, and each is now a rule with a mutation shown to fire, or a restatement.\n\nTHE PAGES QUESTION IS SETTLED AND THE ANSWER WAS ALWAYS IN THE FILE. The consultation raised actions/configure-pages' `enablement` input as a possible way to turn Pages on from a workflow and marked its own suggestion INFERRED, NOT VERIFIED, saying one dispatched run would answer it. The action's action.yml answers it: \"This option requires a token other than `GITHUB_TOKEN`\". No run needed. pages.yml had been giving a narrower reason -- this environment's proxy 403s -- which is true, is about the machine the loop runs on, and left exactly that gap open. decision/0141.\n\nCI CAUGHT TWO FAILURES THIS PASS AND BOTH WERE MINE, both downstream of requiring the field pair. Rule 3's message lists what an entry RECORDS, so the fixture gaining a third pair changed the message two assertions matched exactly; `guards` failed for that one reason and named it precisely -- the witness \"did not pass with the guard in place, so it cannot prove anything about removing it\", which is the registry doing its job. And DocsSiteBuildTest counts `pages: write` as a substring, so the comment quoting configure-pages' manifest made it read 2. THE CHEAP FIX WAS TO REWORD THE COMMENT AND IT WAS THE WRONG ONE: a test that can only be satisfied by making the source less clear about itself has stopped measuring the property. Counted as YAML keys instead, which is what the assertion always meant, and which the continue-on-error assertion eleven lines above had already had to do for the same reason. decision/0143.\n\nAND run/0022's OWN PROSE WAS CITING A MUTATION WITH NO NODE. This description says, above, \"Re-run here: dropping boot-published-image still exits 1, and so does dropping image -> changelog-section.\" The re-run happened; nothing in the graph recorded it, so the claim sat in prose where nothing resolves or checks it -- decision/0080's self-sealing citation, turned on the ledger itself. mutation/0065 is that re-run done first-hand rather than copied forward, and widened from the two edges the prose named to all four assert-release-gate.py requires since PR #366. All four refuse. decision/0142.\n\nTHE SECOND CONSULTATION, AND IT BROKE THE BELIEF THE FIRST ONE LEFT STANDING. After the claims pass landed, every open item's remaining clause read as needing one of three owner actions -- a tag, two Pages settings, a decision on #382 -- and the loop reported that. A Fable agent was asked to attack exactly that claim, at CLAUSE granularity, because item granularity has been wrong seven times here. It came back with two buildable items, two owner actions nobody had written down, and two more findings filed. Every factual claim was checked against the tree before anything was touched; all six held.\n\nTHE ONE THAT MATTERED MOST WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A v1 NOBODY COULD INSTALL. GitHub publishes a new package PRIVATE -- a linked package inherits a repository's permissions but NOT its visibility -- and all four release.yml jobs that pull the published image log in first. Each is individually right; boot-published-image's comment argues it explicitly. Nothing made the assumption a stranger makes. So the v1.0.0 tag would have gone green end to end, this run's own stopping rule would have fired, and the README's `docker run` would have answered `denied`. The word \"visibility\" appeared exactly once in the repository, in the comment explaining why a job does not assume it. PR #410. decision/0146.\n\nTHE OWNER LIST WENT FROM THREE TO FIVE, and decision/0147 records why both were missed structurally rather than carelessly: package visibility cannot be set before the package exists, so it never appeared in any sweep of settings-you-could-set-now; private vulnerability reporting was named in docs/v1-audit.md and nowhere else, and a prose list inside a document is not disposal.\n\nTHE PRODUCT IMAGE WAS BUILDING ITS ASSETS ON NODE 26 while .nvmrc, package.json's engines, pages.yml and a whole CLAUDE.md section say 22. Dependabot walked it 24 -> 26 with a \"*\" group pattern and no decision anywhere. Recorded no wider than the evidence supports: the image job was green, the product was not broken, and the lockfile rewrite CLAUDE.md warns about cannot escape the build stage. PR #411, with two guards that fail at different times.\n\nAND A MUTATION DISCIPLINE CAME OUT OF IT THAT NOTHING HAD WRITTEN DOWN. Of five mutants against the anonymous-pull guard, two passed. The obvious reading -- two weak spots -- was half wrong: one was a real weakness (the check scanned a job's whole run: body for a word that survived in a SHELL COMMENT above the error it was checking), and one was a mutant whose regex matched nothing, so the guard was answering about an intact file. Those are indistinguishable from an exit code and call for opposite fixes. decision/0144: a green result is not evidence until the mutant is shown to have mutated, which is CLAUDE.md's \"a red job is not evidence by itself\" arriving from the other side.\n\nALL FIVE BUILDABLE CONSULTATION FINDINGS ARE ON MAIN, and the sixth is a repository setting. #410 (nothing pulled the published image the way a stranger does), #411 (the image built its assets on Node 26), #413 (the record of both), #414 (spec 13 contradicting itself six lines apart) and #415 (SECURITY.md promising an edit nobody owned). The belief this run opened with -- three owner actions, nothing buildable -- was wrong in both halves.\n\nTHE THROUGH-LINE IS ONE SHAPE, and it is worth naming because it produced five separate pieces of work: something asserted a property that nothing enforced. vocab.md's always-present keys, the absent contiguity rule, LOOP.md's mutability sentence, every release job authenticating, the Dockerfile's node major, spec 13's depends_on bullet, SECURITY.md's future tense. In each case the claim was TRUE when written and nothing kept it true.\n\nAND THE SAME ERROR WAS REPRODUCED TWICE WHILE FIXING IT, both caught before merge. A replacement bullet in spec 13 said \"Healthchecks on every service\" where compose.yaml declares three. A clause written for item/spec-section13-reconcile carried a path-and-line citation into compose.yaml, which the citation guard refused on the very pass that introduced it -- and then refused the rewrite, because that explained the removal by quoting the citation and its pattern cannot tell a citation from a mention of one. Writing about rot is not protection from it.\n\nA DEPENDABOT PULL REQUEST WAS OPEN THE WHOLE TIME AND I REPORTED \"no open PRs\" TWICE. #412 opened at 07:40, mid-cycle, while four of this run's own pull requests were in flight; the reports that missed it were counting what THIS SESSION had opened rather than what the repository had. A scheduled check-in caught it.\n\nTHE DIFF WAS READ BEFORE MERGING, and the review is the part worth keeping: five SHA-pinned action bumps, all major, and TWO OF THEM LAND IN CODE PATHS THAT HAVE NEVER EXECUTED. deploy-pages v4->v5 appears only in pages.yml's `deploy` job, gated on DOCS_SITE_PAGES_ENABLED and skipping; setup-qemu v3->v4 appears only in release.yml, which has never run. Their first execution will be the day Pages is enabled and the day a tag is pushed -- the two runs that already carry the most first-time risk. Merged rather than held, because a stale pin is equally unverified and accumulates; the merge commit names both so whoever cuts the tag knows.\n\nAND RECORDING IT ALMOST INVENTED A CONVENTION. The first attempt gave #412 a PullRequest node, which the validator refused twice -- unsorted identifiers, and an `implements` naming an item this run never touched. The established practice was already there and is the opposite: both previous Dependabot merges, #42 at e8b15f9 and #40 at 001eff0, are recorded as merge entries with `pullRequest: null` and no node at all. A dependency bump discharges no backlog item, so it has nothing to implement. Reading what the ledger already did beat reasoning about what it should do.\n\nTHE BOARD IS EMPTY AND THE REASON IS MECHANICAL, not a matter of priority. Of sixteen items whose dependsOn are satisfied, eight say \"Deferred until after v1.0.0\" in their own doneWhen, five are v1 items needing the tag or a repository setting, and of the remaining three one clause is the owner's, one is blocked on issue #382, and the last -- item/directory-share-permission, well specified and size S -- must not land because main is what gets tagged. decision/0149 records the test that separates it from item/compose-boot-ci, which is equally post-v1 and DID correctly land pre-tag: would tagging today ship this to a self-hoster?",
